%% D2. One user request from start to end: "review this week's new findings".
%% Each column has its own soft colour (as in D1/D3). 🤖 marks an AI call, 👤 a step where a person takes part.
%% The example is generic on purpose; the real lab case (a kernel CVE) is explained in PHASE3.md.
%% Counts ("12 new groups", "30 CVEs") are illustrative, not lab results.
sequenceDiagram
    box rgb(219,234,254) 🏢 Customer network
        participant C as 🔌 Connector
    end
    box rgb(220,252,231) ☁️ Triage service · EU
        participant S as ⚙️ Service
    end
    box rgb(237,233,254) AI · EU
        participant A as 🤖 AI model
    end
    box rgb(255,237,213) Person
        actor P as 👤 Analyst
    end

    Note over C,P: Request: the analyst reviews this week's new findings
    Note over C,S: Every 6 hours, no person involved
    C->>S: 1. New findings (host names → aliases)
    S->>S: 2. Group them by fix and re-apply past decisions

    S->>A: 3. 🤖 "Is this group a real risk?"
    A-->>S: Proposal + evidence + question for the analyst
    S->>S: 4. Check the proposal (evidence exists? exploited CVE?)<br/>If it fails: no AI proposal, the analyst decides
    S->>S: 5. Rank the fix list with fixed rules
    S->>A: 6. 🤖 "Explain each item of the fix list"
    A-->>S: Plain-language reasons (the order does not change)
    S->>P: 7. 📧 "12 new groups to review"

    P->>S: 8. 👤 Signs in
    S-->>P: "Group of 30 CVEs: needs context" + the AI's question
    P->>S: 9. 👤 Confirms or changes the proposal → reason, owner, expiry
    P->>S: 10. 👤 Picks what to patch from the fix list

    Note over C,P: Next sync: the decision is re-applied automatically.<br/>It is reopened if the package changes or the decision expires.
